  "hints": [
    "Base case: for n = 0, 5⁰ − 1 = 0, and 3 divides 0.",
    "Write the hypothesis as an equation, 5^(2k) − 1 = 3m, so you can substitute it. Then 5^(2(k + 1)) − 1 = 25·5^(2k) − 1 = 25(5^(2k) − 1) + 24.",
    "Substitute: 25·3m + 24 = 75m + 24. Factor out 3."
  ],
  "solution": [
    "Base case: for $n = 0$, $5^{0} - 1 = 0 = 3 \\cdot 0$, so $3 \\mid (5^{0} - 1)$.",
    "Inductive step: suppose $3 \\mid (5^{2k} - 1)$ for some $k \\ge 0$, so $5^{2k} - 1 = 3m$ for an integer $m$.",
    "Then $5^{2(k+1)} - 1 = 25 \\cdot 5^{2k} - 1 = 25(5^{2k} - 1) + 24 = 75m + 24 = 3(25m + 8)$, and $25m + 8$ is an integer, so $3 \\mid (5^{2(k+1)} - 1)$.",
    "By induction, $3 \\mid (5^{2n} - 1)$ for every integer $n \\ge 0$. $\\blacksquare$"
  ],
